1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Carbon-13 told you ethanol has two kinds of

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:03,600
carbon.

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,480
Now look at its proton NMR spectrum.

4
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,000
There are three signals, they're different sizes, and

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,000
two of them are split into little clusters

6
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:12,800
of lines.

7
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,760
Each of those features is a separate clue.

8
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:18,400
Read all three, and the spectrum tells you

9
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,960
not just which hydrogens are there, but how

10
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:21,920
they're joined.

11
00:00:23,134 --> 00:00:26,893
Proton NMR works the same way as carbon-13,

12
00:00:26,934 --> 00:00:30,813
but it watches hydrogen-1 nuclei, called protons here.

13
00:00:31,134 --> 00:00:35,613
Electrons shield each hydrogen, nearby electronegative atoms deshield

14
00:00:35,694 --> 00:00:38,413
it, and TMS still sets zero.

15
00:00:38,734 --> 00:00:41,133
The scale is much shorter, though, from 0

16
00:00:41,214 --> 00:00:42,733
to about 12 ppm.

17
00:00:43,374 --> 00:00:45,853
Proton shifts aren't on the data sheet, so

18
00:00:45,934 --> 00:00:48,173
an exam question gives you any values you

19
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:48,653
need.

20
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:52,826
First clue: the number of signals.

21
00:00:53,147 --> 00:00:57,066
Hydrogens in identical surroundings give one signal, so

22
00:00:57,147 --> 00:00:59,946
the signals count the hydrogen environments.

23
00:01:00,027 --> 00:01:02,586
In ethanol, the three hydrogens on the end

24
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:04,826
carbon are all alike, and the two on

25
00:01:04,907 --> 00:01:06,826
the middle carbon are alike.

26
00:01:06,907 --> 00:01:09,466
The O H hydrogen is on its own.

27
00:01:09,547 --> 00:01:12,626
That's three environments, so three signals.

28
00:01:12,987 --> 00:01:15,946
Symmetry merges hydrogens too, just as it merged

29
00:01:16,027 --> 00:01:16,826
carbons.

30
00:01:18,034 --> 00:01:20,593
Second, where each signal sits.

31
00:01:21,074 --> 00:01:22,993
The C H two hydrogens are on the

32
00:01:23,074 --> 00:01:24,673
carbon bonded to oxygen.

33
00:01:25,234 --> 00:01:28,833
The oxygen pulls electrons away, so they're deshielded,

34
00:01:28,914 --> 00:01:31,793
and they appear near 3.7 ppm.

35
00:01:32,114 --> 00:01:34,353
The C H three hydrogens are further from

36
00:01:34,394 --> 00:01:37,073
the oxygen, so they sit near 1.2.

37
00:01:37,634 --> 00:01:40,193
Hydrogens close to an oxygen, a C equals

38
00:01:40,354 --> 00:01:43,713
O or a halogen usually land further left.

39
00:01:44,934 --> 00:01:47,573
Third, how big each signal is.

40
00:01:47,894 --> 00:01:50,773
The area under a signal is proportional to

41
00:01:50,814 --> 00:01:52,933
the number of hydrogens that make it.

42
00:01:53,254 --> 00:01:55,893
The machine draws an integration line that steps

43
00:01:56,014 --> 00:01:58,293
up at each signal, and the step heights

44
00:01:58,374 --> 00:01:59,493
compare the areas.

45
00:02:00,054 --> 00:02:02,693
For ethanol, they come out three to two

46
00:02:02,934 --> 00:02:05,440
to one, matching the C H three, the

47
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:07,333
C H two and the O H.

48
00:02:07,894 --> 00:02:10,213
It's the area that counts, not the height

49
00:02:10,254 --> 00:02:11,333
of the tallest line.

50
00:02:11,814 --> 00:02:14,293
And areas give a ratio, not the actual

51
00:02:14,454 --> 00:02:15,093
numbers.

52
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,100
Now the clusters of lines.

53
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:20,700
Each hydrogen on the next carbon is a

54
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:22,540
tiny magnet too.

55
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,700
It either lines up with the field, adding

56
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:27,340
a little to it, or against it, taking

57
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,500
a little away.

58
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:31,340
Look at the C H three in ethanol.

59
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:33,900
Its two neighbours can be both with, one

60
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:36,380
with and one against, which can happen two

61
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:39,100
ways, or both against.

62
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:42,300
That's three slightly different fields, so the signal

63
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:44,940
splits into three lines, with the middle one

64
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:46,140
twice as tall.

65
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:49,406
That's the n plus one rule.

66
00:02:49,687 --> 00:02:52,406
For simple spectra like these, a hydrogen with

67
00:02:52,567 --> 00:02:55,046
n equivalent neighbours on the next carbon gives

68
00:02:55,287 --> 00:02:56,926
n plus one lines.

69
00:02:57,207 --> 00:02:59,606
The C H three has two neighbours, so

70
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:00,406
it's a triplet.

71
00:03:00,967 --> 00:03:02,886
The C H two has three neighbours on

72
00:03:02,927 --> 00:03:05,206
the methyl side, so it's a quartet.

73
00:03:05,847 --> 00:03:08,406
Hydrogens in the same environment don't split each

74
00:03:08,487 --> 00:03:08,806
other.

75
00:03:09,287 --> 00:03:11,766
And the O H hydrogen swaps between molecules

76
00:03:11,847 --> 00:03:14,326
so fast that it usually shows as one

77
00:03:14,407 --> 00:03:16,966
line, called a singlet, and doesn't split its

78
00:03:17,047 --> 00:03:17,686
neighbours.

79
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,540
Let's read one from scratch.

80
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:24,100
A compound C two H five Br gives

81
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:27,700
two signals, so it has two hydrogen environments.

82
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:30,180
The areas are three to two.

83
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:34,420
The smaller signal, near 3.4, is a quartet,

84
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:36,900
so those two hydrogens have three neighbours.

85
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:40,660
The larger, near 1.7, is a triplet, so

86
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:42,900
those three hydrogens have two neighbours.

87
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:45,220
A C H three next to a C

88
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,780
H two is an ethyl group, so this

89
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:49,300
is bromoethane.

90
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,020
The C H two sits further left because

91
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:53,540
it's bonded to the bromine.

92
00:03:54,767 --> 00:03:56,286
Here's the classic trap.

93
00:03:56,687 --> 00:03:59,566
A quartet doesn't mean four hydrogens.

94
00:03:59,807 --> 00:04:02,526
The number of lines counts the neighbours, plus

95
00:04:02,607 --> 00:04:03,006
one.

96
00:04:03,487 --> 00:04:06,046
The area counts the hydrogens in the signal

97
00:04:06,127 --> 00:04:07,326
itself.

98
00:04:07,447 --> 00:04:11,006
So in bromoethane, the quartet is two hydrogens,

99
00:04:11,087 --> 00:04:13,166
and the triplet is three.

100
00:04:13,287 --> 00:04:16,446
Keep the two questions apart: how many lines,

101
00:04:16,607 --> 00:04:18,126
then how big an area.

102
00:04:19,334 --> 00:04:21,453
Now a molecule with a surprise.

103
00:04:21,734 --> 00:04:25,093
Ethyl ethanoate gives three signals, in the ratio

104
00:04:25,254 --> 00:04:27,253
three to two to three.

105
00:04:27,574 --> 00:04:30,453
The quartet near 4.1 and the triplet near

106
00:04:30,534 --> 00:04:33,653
1.3 are the ethyl group, and the quartet

107
00:04:33,734 --> 00:04:36,453
sits far left because its carbon is bonded

108
00:04:36,494 --> 00:04:37,973
to the ester oxygen.

109
00:04:38,294 --> 00:04:41,493
The third signal, near 2.0, is a singlet

110
00:04:41,574 --> 00:04:42,853
with three hydrogens.

111
00:04:43,414 --> 00:04:45,573
A C H three with no hydrogens on

112
00:04:45,654 --> 00:04:48,053
the next atom has nothing to split it,

113
00:04:48,134 --> 00:04:50,213
and this one sits beside the C equals

114
00:04:50,294 --> 00:04:50,693
O.

115
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,900
One line for the three clues: signals, areas,

116
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,340
neighbours plus one.

117
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:02,540
Signals count environments, areas compare hydrogens, and lines

118
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:04,940
count equivalent neighbours, plus one.

119
00:05:06,167 --> 00:05:06,806
Your turn.

120
00:05:07,047 --> 00:05:10,966
Predict the proton NMR spectrum of methyl propanoate.

121
00:05:11,447 --> 00:05:14,086
Give the number of signals, their relative areas

122
00:05:14,327 --> 00:05:16,646
and the splitting of each, with reasons.

123
00:05:17,207 --> 00:05:18,646
Pause here to answer.

124
00:05:29,067 --> 00:05:32,106
Three signals, in the ratio three to two

125
00:05:32,187 --> 00:05:32,906
to three.

126
00:05:33,187 --> 00:05:35,386
The C H three on the oxygen has

127
00:05:35,467 --> 00:05:38,466
no hydrogens next door, so it's a singlet.

128
00:05:38,747 --> 00:05:41,226
The C H two has three neighbours, so

129
00:05:41,387 --> 00:05:43,626
it's a quartet, and the end C H

130
00:05:43,787 --> 00:05:46,146
three has two, so it's a triplet.

131
00:05:46,427 --> 00:05:49,066
Counting the methyl hydrogens across the ester link

132
00:05:49,147 --> 00:05:51,626
as neighbours is the slip: they're not on

133
00:05:51,707 --> 00:05:52,746
the next carbon.

134
00:05:53,967 --> 00:05:54,606
Last one.

135
00:05:55,167 --> 00:05:58,446
A compound C five H ten O gives

136
00:05:58,527 --> 00:06:02,126
just two proton signals: a triplet at 1.05

137
00:06:02,287 --> 00:06:06,846
ppm with relative area 3, and a quartet

138
00:06:07,007 --> 00:06:11,286
at 2.44 ppm with relative area 2.

139
00:06:11,647 --> 00:06:13,726
Identify the compound from the four on the

140
00:06:13,807 --> 00:06:15,726
card, and justify your answer.

141
00:06:16,287 --> 00:06:17,566
Pause here to answer.

142
00:06:27,967 --> 00:06:29,806
It's pentan-3-one.

143
00:06:30,047 --> 00:06:31,966
A triplet and a quartet in a three

144
00:06:32,047 --> 00:06:34,646
to two ratio suggest an ethyl group.

145
00:06:34,927 --> 00:06:37,566
But the formula has ten hydrogens, so the

146
00:06:37,687 --> 00:06:40,446
real numbers are six and four, which means

147
00:06:40,527 --> 00:06:42,846
two identical ethyl groups.

148
00:06:42,927 --> 00:06:46,766
Of these candidates, only the symmetrical pentan-3-one gives

149
00:06:46,847 --> 00:06:50,686
two signals, and the quartet at 2.44 fits

150
00:06:50,767 --> 00:06:52,686
a C H two next to the C

151
00:06:52,847 --> 00:06:53,646
equals O.

152
00:06:53,967 --> 00:06:56,686
Reading three to two as five hydrogens, and

153
00:06:56,767 --> 00:06:58,766
one ethyl group, is the trap.

154
00:06:59,967 --> 00:07:03,846
So, with a proton spectrum, ask four questions.

155
00:07:04,127 --> 00:07:05,326
How many signals?

156
00:07:05,567 --> 00:07:07,086
Where does each one sit?

157
00:07:07,327 --> 00:07:08,926
How big is each area?

158
00:07:09,007 --> 00:07:10,486
And how many lines?

159
00:07:10,767 --> 00:07:14,006
Then build a structure that explains every one.

160
00:07:14,367 --> 00:07:18,046
Next, you'll combine mass spectrometry, infrared and both

161
00:07:18,127 --> 00:07:21,486
kinds of NMR to identify a single molecule.

162
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:24,140
Here are the key notes.

163
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:26,300
Pause the video if you need more time

164
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:27,180
to copy them.
